In this lesson we'll look at two really handy shortcuts, the remainder theorem and the factor theorem, that save you from doing long division every time. Here's the key idea. When you divide a polynomial by a linear factor like x minus a, the leftover piece, called the remainder, is just the value of the polynomial at a. That is, you plug in a and whatever number comes out is the remainder. No division needed. Now here's the cool part. If that value happens to be zero, then x minus a is a factor of the polynomial, and a is a root. That's the factor theorem. So testing whether something divides evenly is as simple as substituting a and checking if you get zero. One quick sign watch. If the factor is written as x plus three, rewrite it as x minus negative three, so you substitute negative three. And if the factor is bx minus a, the root is a divided by b. Keep those in mind and you're set. Now let's see it in action.